""
0 -1 99303 0 6
E
"door ironwood plank"
"@WThe huge doors stand easily thirty feet tall, and are highly polished,

enhancing the natural colour and glow of the metallic ironwood.  Each of them

is formed from a single piece of this prized wood, and must have been

harvested from a tree thousands of years old.

"
E
"mosaic floor gems stones"
"This mosaic is formed from mainly deep blue and green gems and precious

stones, and what appears to be gold filigree, which has somehow survived the

years and the passage of thousands across its surface.  The pattern depicts a

large structure bathed in the light of the gods.

"
A
""
""
"continue;

"
E
"to_char"
"@WYou descend the grey marble steps and leave the cathedral.

"
!
3 0 4
-1
S

#99302
"Old Cathedral - Entrance Hall"
"In the eastern end of the hall, there is little evidence that the cathedral

has aged or changed at all.  All here seems to be almost exactly as it should

be.  The mosaics on the floor here, made of the same gems and stones as those

before the main door, are still full of life and colour.  The walls are of the

same granite as those on the outside of the building, and stained-glass

windows are set high into the walls, casting coruscating lights on the floor

and walls.

"
""
301465600 0
0 6 0
D3
""
""
0 -1 99301 0 6
E
"stained glass window wall"
"@WTwo stained-glass windows are set high into the walls here.  One depicts a

robed figure surrounded with an aura of light; the other is of a large

structure which could well be this very cathedral.

"
E
"floor gems stones mosaic"
"The mosaic on the floor here depicts a benevolent, kindly looking old man.  He

is holding a key in his left hand, and a book in his right.

"
-1
S

#99303
"Old Cathedral - Entrance Hall"
"The western part of this hall is dank and musty, where a leak has formed high

up on the wall.  The mosaic on the floor is dulled and grimy from pooled water

stagnating over the years.  In the corners of the hall, leaves have become

trapped in corners after having been blown in by gusts of wind.  To the east,

the hall continues and the main doors to the cathedral are visible.

"
""
301465600 0
0 6 0
D1
""
""
0 -1 99301 0 6
E
"leaves corners"
"Piles of leaves rest in the corners, having been blown in by wind over the

years.  They rest in piles of dust - the remains of their predecessors.

"
E
"mosaic floor"
"The mosaic here is obscured where water has stagnated and calcified over the

years.  It is impossible to discern what it once depicted.

"
E
"west wall leak"
"A leak has formed here, tingeing the wall with a green mold.

"
-1
S

